DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260807
DESCRIPTION:An article published in the April 30\, 1819 edition of the Rale
 igh Register and North Carolina Gazette proclaimed the following: “It is
  not probably known to many of our readers\, that the citizens of Mecklenb
 urg County\, in this State\, made a Declaration of Independence more than 
 a year before Congress made theirs. The following Document on the subject 
 has lately come to the hands of the Editor from unquestionable authority\,
   and is published that it may go down to posterity.”  \n\nThe document 
 in question was called the Mecklenburg Declaration of Independence\, and i
 t had allegedly declared Mecklenburg “free and independent” from Briti
 sh rule on May 20\, 1775. Of the 27 men who were said to have signed the d
 ocument\, almost all were Presbyterian.\n\nBut many people were confused 
 – why were they just now hearing about this revolutionary action? The or
 iginal document burned in a house fire in 1800\, and the published version
  had been reconstructed from notes made by the clerk of the 1775 meeting. 
 No extant copies could be found.\n\nThe debate over the authenticity of th
 e Mecklenburg Declaration began. And\, when a copy of a document called th
 e Mecklenburg Resolves written on May 30\, 1775\, was discovered in a 1775
  newspaper\, the debate intensified. The Resolves were revolutionary\, but
  stopped short of declaring independence. Did both documents exist? Or was
  the Declaration being conflated with the Resolves?\n\nExamine the evidenc
 e and decide for yourself by visiting the new exhibit\, “Mecklenburg Dec
 laration?” at the Presbyterian Heritage Center.\n\nThe PHC is open Tuesd
 ay-Friday (10-4) and Saturday (12-4).
GEO:35.646737;-82.300463
LOCATION:Presbyterian Heritage Center
SUMMARY:Mecklenburg's Declaration: NC's Revolutionary Presbyterians
URL;VALUE=URI:https://events.dncr.nc.gov/event/mecklenburgs-declaration-ncs
 -revolutionary-presbyterians
CATEGORIES:Exhibits & Tours
CATEGORIES:Exhibitions
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:NC Cultural Resources
DTSTAMP:20260731T040428Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_52949644644151
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260807
DESCRIPTION:Save the Date!\n\nNC’s Route to Victory in the Revolution: Th
 e Race to the Dan\nAugust 7–8\, 2026\n\nJoin us for a two-day commemorat
 ion exploring North Carolina’s critical role in the Revolutionary War du
 ring the Winter Campaign of 1781. Through keynote presentations\, living h
 istory\, reenactments\, and historical interpretation\, this event will hi
 ghlight the events and people connected to “The Race to the Dan\,” a p
 ivotal campaign that helped lead to the British surrender at Yorktown late
 r that year.\n\nFriday\, August 7\, 2026\nBethania\, North Carolina\n\nThe
  opening day will feature a historical program and keynote address in the 
 historic Moravian community of Bethania\, followed by a reception at the C
 ornwallis House (c. 1770).\n\nFeatured Historians:\n• Dr. Wayne Lee\, UN
 C–Chapel Hill\, West Point Military Chair\n• Dr. Zachary Deibel\, Virg
 inia Military Institute\n\nSaturday\, August 8\, 2026\nShallow Ford State 
 Historic Site – Lewisville\, North Carolina\n\nThe second day will inclu
 de reenactments\, living history demonstrations\, a keynote address\, and 
 luncheon programming at the historic crossing site of Shallow Ford.\n\nFea
 tured Historian:\n• Dr. Troy Kickler\, NC Semiquincentennial Committee\n
 \nSponsored by the Bethania Historical Association\, The Conservation Fund
 \, Lewisville Historical Society\, and the NC Society of Cincinnati\, with
  assistance from the NC Historic Sites Division.\n\nMore details to come.
GEO:36.180889;-80.339793
LOCATION:Bethania Historical Association
SUMMARY:NC's Route to Victory in the Revolution: The Race to the Dan
URL;VALUE=URI:https://events.dncr.nc.gov/event/ncs-route-to-victory-in-the-
 revolution-the-race-to-the-dan
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:NC Cultural Resources
