DTSTAMP:20260731T040432Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_51619879291363
DTSTART:20261114T150000Z
DTEND:20261114T173000Z
DESCRIPTION:The Indigenous Workshop - America 250 NC\,  introduces beginner
 s to the key steps and records that can unlock the  history of Black India
 ns. Whether you’ve never started your search or you’ve hit a roadblock
 \, you’ll learn how to make progress with confidence.\n\nIn this Native 
 American History Month presentation\, you’ll learn how to:\n\nBegin your
  family history journey using records that are free and easy to access onl
 ine.Find ancestors’ Tribal Rolls and Native American Records — and und
 erstand what those records reveal.Use clues from 1870 and beyond to trace 
 family lines further back in time.Overcome challenges created by the Trail
 s of Tears\, Slavery\,  and Segregation-era record keeping.You’ll see ho
 w ordinary documents — from Rolls and Census lists to Freedmen’s Burea
 u papers —   can tell extraordinary family stories.\n\nDeLoatch shares p
 ractical examples from his own research\, showing how he traced his ancest
 ry from Africa back 400 years to relatives enslaved in America.   Sponsore
 d by the AFRO-AMERICAN HISTORICAL & GENEALOGICAL SOCIETY\, INC (AAHGS\, In
 c.)\, North Carolina/Piedmont-Triad Chapter
GEO:36.075685;-79.787776
LOCATION:Greensboro History Museum\, Auditorium
SUMMARY:BLACK INDIANS: "Native American Family Research"
URL;VALUE=URI:https://events.dncr.nc.gov/event/black-indians-native-america
 n-family-research
CATEGORIES:Education Programs
CATEGORIES:Workshops & Trainings
CATEGORIES:Lectures & Talks
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:NC Cultural Resources
DTSTAMP:20260731T040432Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_53138242000944
DTSTART:20261114T150000Z
DTEND:20261114T220000Z
DESCRIPTION:Community Stories: Sustaining North Carolina’s Black Heritage
  at the Gregg Museum of Art & Design presents a series of vignettes explor
 ing spaces of dignity\, belonging\, safety\, and imagination within Black 
 communities across North Carolina. Featuring historical photographs\, cont
 emporary images\, artworks from the Gregg Museum collection including a ha
 ndmade guitar by Freeman Vines\, and ephemera from community partners\, th
 e exhibition reflects on place\, memory\, creativity\, and spiritual resil
 ience across generations. Through stories of triumph and adversity\, Commu
 nity Stories honors the ways Black Americans have sustained and uplifted t
 heir communities through family\, entrepreneurship\, cultural expression\,
  and soulful imagination\, while inspiring future generations.
GEO:35.784925;-78.661662
LOCATION:Gregg Museum of Art & Design
SUMMARY:Community Stories: Sustaining North Carolina’s Black Heritage
URL;VALUE=URI:https://events.dncr.nc.gov/event/community-stories-sustaining
 -north-carolinas-black-heritage
